## Why This Is Asked
Designing a modular, policy-driven prompt assembly system tests understanding of multi-tenant safety, latency budgets, and deterministic prompt composition under constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Fragment catalogs with metadata: policy, latency, locale, tone
- Tenant-aware scoring and ordering under latency limits
- Guardrails and watermark injection when needed
- Audit logging with fragment IDs and decision trace

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype outline (not full implementation)
const fragments = [
  {id:'intent', text:'Explain concisely', latency:10},
  {id:'tone', text:'in a formal style', latency:5},
  {id:'locale', text:'English', latency:2}
];
function assemble(tenantPolicy, budget){
  // simplistic greedy selection
  let chosen = [];
  let used= new Set();
  for(const f of fragments){
    if(!used.has(f.id) && budget- f.latency >=0 && tenantPolicy.includes(f.id)){
      chosen.push(f); used.add(f.id);
      budget -= f.latency;
    }
    if(chosen.length>=3) break;
  }
  return chosen.map(x=>x.text).join(' ');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle non-determinism in fragment selection across model updates?
- How to validate audits for compliance and GDPR logging obligations?

## Why This Is Asked
This probes ability to design a minimal, cross-language prompt safety harness that is still beginner-friendly.

## Key Concepts
- Multilingual prompt handling
- Lightweight dictionary-based translation
- Safety checks for prompts and patterns
- Per-language reporting and remediation
- JSON payload design and edge cases

## Code Example
```javascript
function testPrompt(enPrompt) {
  const dict = { es: { export: "exportar", emails: "correo" }, fr: { export: "exporter", emails: "courriel" }, zh: { export: "导出", emails: "电子邮件" } };
  const langs = ["es","fr","zh"];
  const unsafe = ["system prompt","secret","privileged"];
  const translate = (p, lang) => {
    let t = p;
    Object.entries(dict[lang] || {}).forEach(([enWord, trans]) => {
      t = t.replace(new RegExp("\\b" + enWord + "\\b","gi"), trans);
    });
    return t;
  };
  const results = langs.map(l => {
    const t = translate(enPrompt, l);
    const issues = unsafe.filter(s => t.toLowerCase().includes(s));
    return { lang: l, prompt: t, ok: issues.length === 0, issues };
  });
  return { original: enPrompt, results };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale to more languages and handle ambiguous terms?
- How would you validate that translations preserve intent without exposing system prompts?

